    { q: "In what sense is entropy a property of a description rather than of a system?", a: "S = k log W counts the microstates consistent with a specified macrostate, so a single exact microstate has no entropy of its own. Once you fix which macrovariables you are tracking, the count is objective. Whether that choice is dictated by physics or by the observer's interests is still disputed." },
    { q: "What does Zermelo's recurrence objection establish, and why does it not matter in practice?", a: "Poincare recurrence guarantees a bounded system returns arbitrarily close to its initial state, so entropy cannot increase monotonically forever. Boltzmann conceded the point and estimated the recurrence time for a macroscopic gas at around ten to the power of ten to the twenty-third seconds. It refutes the second law as an exceptionless law while leaving it perfectly reliable on any physical timescale." },
    { q: "How do fluctuation theorems change the status of the second law?", a: "They convert a prohibition into an exact probability statement. Crooks gives the ratio of forward to reverse trajectory probabilities as the exponential of the entropy produced over k. Entropy decreases are exponentially improbable in the entropy involved, so they are unobservable at macroscopic scale but common for micron-sized objects, as single-molecule experiments confirm." },
    { q: "What is the link between Landauer's principle and our ability to know the past?", a: "A record is a low-entropy correlation between something now and an event earlier, and creating or resetting one is logically irreversible, so it costs at least kT ln 2 per bit in dissipated heat. Records are therefore thermodynamic objects that can only be laid down in the direction of increasing entropy. That is why the past leaves traces and the future does not, and why memory and causal inference run one way." }
